    id:`fl01`, cat:`Life Sciences`, title:`Cell Structure & Organelles`, diff:`Easy`,
    qs:[
      { q:`What is the basic structural and functional unit of all living things?`, ch:[`The atom`,`The cell`,`The organ`,`The tissue`], ans:1, exp:`The cell theory states that the cell is the smallest unit that can carry out all the functions of life — every living organism is made of one or more cells.` },
      { q:`Which organelle is often called the "powerhouse of the cell"?`, ch:[`Nucleus`,`Ribosome`,`Mitochondrion`,`Golgi apparatus`], ans:2, exp:`Mitochondria break down glucose in the presence of oxygen to produce ATP, the cell's main energy currency — hence the "powerhouse" nickname.` },
      { q:`What is the main function of the nucleus?`, ch:[`Producing ATP`,`Storing the cell's DNA and controlling gene expression`,`Building proteins`,`Breaking down waste`], ans:1, exp:`The nucleus houses the cell's genetic material (DNA) and acts as the control center, regulating which genes are turned on or off.` },
      { q:`Which structure is responsible for building proteins in a cell?`, ch:[`Ribosome`,`Lysosome`,`Vacuole`,`Cell wall`], ans:0, exp:`Ribosomes read messenger RNA and assemble amino acids into proteins — they can float free in the cytoplasm or be attached to the rough endoplasmic reticulum.` },
      { q:`What does the cell membrane do?`, ch:[`Produces energy`,`Controls what enters and exits the cell`,`Stores genetic information`,`Breaks down old organelles`], ans:1, exp:`The cell membrane is selectively permeable — it regulates the movement of substances like nutrients, water, and waste into and out of the cell.` },
    ],
