    qs:[
      { q:`What is the primary function of the respiratory system?`, ch:[`Digesting food`,`Exchanging oxygen and carbon dioxide between the air and the blood`,`Filtering blood`,`Producing red blood cells`], ans:1, exp:`The respiratory system brings oxygen into the body and removes carbon dioxide, a waste product of cellular respiration, through gas exchange in the lungs.` },
      { q:`Where does gas exchange actually occur in the lungs?`, ch:[`The trachea`,`The bronchi`,`The alveoli — tiny air sacs surrounded by capillaries`,`The larynx`], ans:2, exp:`Alveoli are the lungs' tiny, thin-walled air sacs where oxygen diffuses into surrounding capillaries and carbon dioxide diffuses out, ready to be exhaled.` },
      { q:`What muscle plays the biggest role in driving breathing?`, ch:[`The bicep`,`The diaphragm`,`The heart`,`The trapezius`], ans:1, exp:`The diaphragm, a dome-shaped muscle beneath the lungs, contracts and flattens to pull air in, then relaxes to help push air out.` },
      { q:`What path does air take from the nose/mouth to the lungs?`, ch:[`Nose/mouth -> trachea -> bronchi -> alveoli`,`Nose/mouth -> alveoli -> trachea -> bronchi`,`Nose/mouth -> esophagus -> lungs`,`Nose/mouth -> diaphragm -> trachea`], ans:0, exp:`Air travels down the trachea (windpipe), which splits into two bronchi (one per lung), which branch further into smaller bronchioles ending in the alveoli.` },
      { q:`Why does gas exchange happen so efficiently in the alveoli?`, ch:[`They have extremely thick walls`,`They are surrounded by muscle tissue instead of capillaries`,`They have thin walls and a huge surface area in close contact with capillaries`,`They contain no blood vessels at all`], ans:2, exp:`Alveoli have very thin walls and, collectively, an enormous surface area, both of which maximize the rate of gas diffusion between air and blood.` },
    ],
